{
  "title": "Virtual threads: what actually changes",
  "content": "There's a gap between how java virtual is taught and how it actually behaves under load. Most write-ups stop exactly where the interesting part starts. Here's what a colleague hitting this for the first time needs to know.\n\nJava virtual threads (JEP 444, final in JDK 21) are the first change to Java's threading model that genuinely alters how you write blocking network services. Yet most teams I talk to either expect a free performance upgrade or dismiss them as a niche feature. The confusion is understandable: virtual threads look like threads, behave like threads in a debugger, and yet the rules for using them well are different from the rules for platform threads. This post clears up what changes, what doesn't, and where the sharp edges are when you put them in a production service that talks to databases, HTTP APIs, and message brokers.\n\nThe real problem this post solves: helping you decide, with evidence from your own service, whether virtual threads will simplify your code or just move your bottleneck from threads to connections.\n\n## The one-sentence version\n\nVirtual threads make thread-per-request cheap again, but they don't make your downstream dependencies cheaper. If your service is limited by database connections, HTTP client pools, or CPU, virtual threads will not increase throughput; they will just change where the queue forms.\n\nThat is the whole story in miniature. Everything below is about the consequences.\n\n## What virtual threads actually are (and aren't)\n\nA virtual thread is a Java thread scheduled by the JDK, not by the operating system. The JDK runs many virtual threads on a small pool of carrier threads (by default, one per CPU core, though the `jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism` system property can change that). When a virtual thread blocks on a supported operation, the JDK unmounts it from its carrier, freeing the carrier to run another virtual thread. When the operation completes, the virtual thread is scheduled again.\n\nThat unmounting is the key. It only works when the blocking call is one the JDK knows how to handle. The list is wide but not universal: `Socket` I/O, `java.nio` channels, `Thread.sleep`, `Object.wait`, most `java.util.concurrent` locks and synchronizers, and `java.net.http.HttpClient` all cooperate. Blocking on `synchronized` used to pin the carrier thread in early JDK 21 builds; JDK 24 (JEP 491) removed that limitation, so a virtual thread blocked inside a `synchronized` block no longer holds its carrier. If you are on JDK 21 or 22, this matters a lot and is the single most common source of \"virtual threads made things worse\" reports.\n\nWhat virtual threads are not:\n\n- They are not a replacement for reactive programming in every case. They replace the *thread pool* part of the problem, not the *backpressure* part.\n- They are not faster per request. A virtual thread that does the same work as a platform thread takes the same time.\n- They are not a thread pool. You should not pool virtual threads. Create one per task; they are cheap (a few hundred bytes of heap, not a megabyte of stack).\n- They do not make `ThreadLocal` free. Each virtual thread still has its own thread-locals, and a service with thousands of virtual threads and a fat `ThreadLocal` cache can use a lot of memory. JDK 21 added `ScopedValue` as a preview (finalized later) specifically to address this, but `ThreadLocal` still works and still costs.\n\n## The mental model: threads are now cheap, connections are not\n\nThink of it like a restaurant. Platform threads are tables: you have a fixed number, and if all tables are full, new customers wait outside. Virtual threads are more tables, effectively unlimited, so nobody waits at the door. But the kitchen (your database, your downstream API) still has a fixed number of burners. Adding tables doesn't add burners. It just means more people are seated, staring at the kitchen, wondering why their food is late.\n\nThis is why the first thing to check when you adopt virtual threads is not your thread count. It is every pool between your service and the work:\n\n- The JDBC connection pool (HikariCP, for example). Default `maximumPoolSize` is 10.\n- The HTTP client connection pool. `java.net.http.HttpClient` has its own limits, and so does every HTTP client library.\n- The Redis client pool (Lettuce, Jedis).\n- Any semaphore or bulkhead you added to protect a downstream.\n\nIf you had 200 platform threads and a Hikari pool of 20, your service was already serializing on the pool. Virtual threads don't change that. They just make the serialization point more obvious, because now you have 10,000 virtual threads all waiting on the same 20 connections.\n\n## Worked example: a blocking HTTP service, before and after\n\nSuppose a service handles requests by calling two downstream HTTP APIs and one database query. Each downstream call takes, illustratively, 50 ms; the database query takes 20 ms. The service is CPU-light. Assume a 4-core box.\n\n**Before (platform threads, fixed pool of 200):**\n\n```java\nExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200);\n```\n\nEach request occupies a platform thread for roughly 120 ms of wall time, almost all of it waiting. With 200 threads, the theoretical maximum throughput is 200 / 0.120 s ≈ 1,666 requests/s, assuming the downstreams can keep up. If they can't, the pool fills and requests queue. The queue is invisible until latency spikes.\n\n**After (virtual threads, one per request):**\n\n```java\ntry (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {\n    server.setExecutor(executor);\n    server.start();\n}\n```\n\nNow the thread count is not the limit. The limit is whichever downstream saturates first. If the Hikari pool is 10 and the database query takes 20 ms, the database caps you at 10 / 0.020 s = 500 requests/s, regardless of how many virtual threads you create. The HTTP client pool might cap you higher or lower. The first saturated dependency sets your ceiling.\n\nThis is why \"we switched to virtual threads and throughput didn't change\" is a common report. The thread pool was never the bottleneck; it was just the most visible one. Virtual threads moved the queue from the thread pool to the connection pool, where it is harder to see.\n\n**A more interesting case:** if your service was previously limited to 200 concurrent requests and each request blocked on a slow downstream (say 2 seconds), you were capped at 100 requests/s. With virtual threads, you can have thousands of in-flight requests, and your throughput is now limited by the downstream's capacity, which might be 500 requests/s. You get a real improvement, but only because the thread pool was the binding constraint. If the downstream was already the constraint, nothing changes except the shape of your queue.\n\n## What changes in your code (and what doesn't)\n\n**What doesn't change:** your blocking code stays blocking. That is the point. You do not need to rewrite your service as reactive or async. A method that calls `jdbcTemplate.query(...)` and then `httpClient.send(...)` still works, and now it can run on a virtual thread without consuming a platform thread for the duration.\n\n**What changes:**\n\n1. **Thread pools become per-task executors.** Replace `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)` with `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` for request handling. Do not pool virtual threads yourself; the executor creates a new one per task.\n\n2. **Thread-local caches need rethinking.** A `ThreadLocal<byte[]>` buffer of 10 KB, multiplied by 10,000 virtual threads, is 100 MB of heap. On 200 platform threads it was 2 MB. Audit your thread-locals before you scale concurrency.\n\n3. **Pinning becomes a debugging concern.** On JDK 21 and 22, a virtual thread that blocks inside a `synchronized` block pins its carrier. If enough virtual threads pin, you can starve the carrier pool and see latency spikes that look like thread starvation. The JFR event `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` (with a configurable threshold) is how you find these. On JDK 24+, this specific cause is gone, but native code that blocks (some JNI calls, some file I/O on older JDKs) can still pin.\n\n4. **`ThreadLocal`-based frameworks need checking.** Some older frameworks (logging, transaction management, security contexts) rely on thread-locals and assume a bounded number of threads. Most modern versions are fine, but the ones that cache per-thread objects are worth a look.\n\n5. **Structured concurrency becomes natural.** `StructuredTaskScope` (preview in JDK 21, still preview in later releases) is designed to pair with virtual threads: fork a subtask per downstream call, join, handle failures together. You don't need it to use virtual threads, but it is the idiomatic way to fan out.\n\n## The pinning trap, specifically\n\nPinning is the failure mode most likely to bite a team moving to virtual threads on JDK 21. Here is the mechanism: a virtual thread running inside a `synchronized` block cannot unmount when it blocks. It holds its carrier thread until the block exits. If you have, say, 4 carrier threads and 100 virtual threads all blocked inside `synchronized` on a slow I/O call, the scheduler has no free carriers and everything stalls.\n\nA typical shape that triggers this:\n\n```java\npublic synchronized String fetch(String key) throws IOException {\n    // blocking I/O inside a synchronized method\n    return httpClient.send(request, BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();\n}\n```\n\nOn JDK 21, every virtual thread calling `fetch` pins a carrier for the duration of the HTTP call. With enough concurrency, the carrier pool is exhausted and unrelated virtual threads stop making progress.\n\nThe fix is not to remove `synchronized` everywhere; it is to avoid blocking I/O inside it. Use `ReentrantLock` (which does not pin) or restructure so the lock is held only around in-memory state. On JDK 24+, the `synchronized` case is fixed, but the general rule still applies to native blocking calls.\n\nTo find pinning, enable JFR with the `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` event and a threshold, for example:\n\n```bash\njava -XX:StartFlightRecording:filename=recording.jfr \\\n     -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full \\\n     -jar service.jar\n```\n\n`jdk.tracePinnedThreads` prints stack traces when a virtual thread pins; it is a diagnostic, not something to leave on in production. The JFR event is the production-safe option.\n\n## Common misreadings\n\n**\"Virtual threads make my service faster.\"** They make it cheaper to have many concurrent requests. Per-request latency is unchanged unless the thread pool was the bottleneck. If your p99 is dominated by a slow downstream, virtual threads will not fix it.\n\n**\"I should replace my reactive stack with virtual threads.\"** Sometimes yes, sometimes no. Virtual threads remove the need for callback-based async in many services, which is a real simplification. But reactive frameworks also give you backpressure, which virtual threads do not. If your service needs to signal a producer to slow down, you still need a mechanism for that (bounded queues, semaphores, reactive streams). Virtual threads make the code look synchronous; they do not make the problem synchronous.\n\n**\"I need to pool virtual threads.\"** No. Pooling defeats the purpose. Create one per task and let the JDK schedule them.\n\n**\"Virtual threads are only for new code.\"** They work with existing blocking code, which is the main appeal. The migration is mostly about auditing thread-locals and pinning, not rewriting business logic.\n\n**\"`Thread.sleep` and `Object.wait` are the problem.\"** They are supported and unmount correctly. The problem cases are pinning (synchronized on older JDKs, native blocking) and anything that holds a carrier while waiting.\n\n## A decision framework for your service\n\nBefore you flip the executor, answer these in order:\n\n1. **Is the thread pool currently the bottleneck?** Measure in-flight requests versus pool size. If the pool is rarely full, virtual threads will not help throughput. They may still simplify code, which is a valid reason.\n\n2. **What is the first downstream to saturate?** Check connection pool sizes and timeouts for JDBC, HTTP, and cache clients. The smallest of these sets your ceiling. Virtual threads do not raise it.\n\n3. **Are there blocking calls inside `synchronized` on JDK 21/22?** If yes, either upgrade to JDK 24+ or refactor to `ReentrantLock`. Run with JFR pinning events in staging to confirm.\n\n4. **Are there fat thread-locals?** Estimate heap: thread-local size × expected concurrent virtual threads. If that number is uncomfortable, switch to `ScopedValue` where possible or shrink the cached object.\n\n5. **Does anything rely on a bounded thread count?** Some rate limiters, metrics, and frameworks assume a fixed pool. Check them.\n\nIf the answers are \"pool not the bottleneck, downstream is,\" virtual threads are a code-simplicity change, not a throughput change. Adopt them for readability, but do not expect a capacity win.\n\n## Quick reference\n\n| Concern | Platform threads | Virtual threads |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cost per thread | ~1 MB stack (default) | A few hundred bytes, grows as needed |\n| Pooling | Required | Not recommended; create per task |\n| Blocking I/O | Consumes a thread | Unmounts, frees carrier (if supported) |\n| `synchronized` + blocking | Fine | Pins carrier on JDK 21/22; fixed in JDK 24 |\n| Thread-locals | Bounded by pool size | Multiply by concurrent virtual threads |\n| Backpressure | Implicit via pool size | Must be explicit (semaphores, bounded queues) |\n| Debugging | Thread dumps show all | Thread dumps show virtual threads; JFR for pinning |\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Do virtual threads work with Spring Boot and Tomcat?**\n\nYes, on recent versions. Spring Boot 3.2 added a configuration property (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`) that switches Tomcat's request executor to virtual threads. Tomcat 10.1+ supports virtual threads as an executor. The caveats are the same as anywhere: check your JDBC pool size, audit thread-locals, and watch for pinning on JDK 21/22. Spring's transaction and security contexts use thread-locals, but they are per-request and bounded, so the multiplication concern is usually manageable.\n\n**Should I set a maximum on virtual threads?**\n\nNot at the thread level. Virtual threads are cheap enough that limiting them is the wrong control. What you should limit is the thing they contend for: database connections, HTTP connections, and any downstream with a quota. Use a `Semaphore` or a bounded pool around the downstream call, not around the thread. This is the same discipline you would apply with platform threads, just made explicit.\n\n**Why did my latency get worse after switching?**\n\nThe most common causes, in order: (1) pinning on JDK 21/22 from `synchronized` around blocking calls, which starves carriers; (2) a connection pool that was already saturated, now with a much longer queue of waiters; (3) thread-local memory pressure causing GC pauses; (4) a framework that assumed a bounded thread count and now sees thousands. JFR with `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` and a thread dump will usually distinguish these quickly.\n\n**Can I use virtual threads with `CompletableFuture`?**\n\nYes, but be deliberate. `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync` without an executor uses the common ForkJoinPool, which is sized to CPU cores and is not a virtual-thread executor. Pass `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` explicitly if you want the task to run on a virtual thread. Mixing virtual threads with callback-based async is legal but tends to produce code that is neither simple nor fast; prefer structured concurrency or plain blocking calls on virtual threads.\n\n## The next 30 minutes\n\nOpen the configuration for your JDBC connection pool (for HikariCP, look for `maximumPoolSize`; for Spring Boot, `spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size`) and write down the number. Then compare it to your current request-handling thread pool size. If the connection pool is smaller, that number, not your thread count, is your real concurrency ceiling, and it is the first thing to reason about before you adopt virtual threads.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Redis documentation](https://redis.io/docs/)\n- [Google SRE Book: Monitoring Distributed Systems](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
